[NAME]
daykeep \- sum and track time in decimal days

[EXAMPLES]
.nf
.RB "$ " "daykeep .9 \- .1"
\&.2000
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-\-hm '09:00 \- 10:30' 11:00 \- 11:45"
1h 30m
2h 15m
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-s \-\-format=minutes \-f today.log"
135
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-\-now"
09761.4564
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-\-convert .5 +9 10:00"
2026\-09\-22 09:00:00 \-0300
2026\-09\-29 21:00:00 \-0300
09761.5417
.RB "$ " "daykeep \-t \-a work.log"
.fi
.PP
The last one tracks time interactively and keeps the entries in
.IR work.log ,
which
.B "daykeep \-f work.log"
sums.

[ENVIRONMENT]
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone for HH:MM input and for dates and times printed by
.BR \-\-convert ,
unless
.B \-u
is given.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must come before the operands.
.TP
.B DAYKEEP_EPOCH
Day 0, unless
.B \-\-epoch
is given; it overrides the config file.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory holding
.IR daykeep/config .
.TP
.B DAYKEEP_NOW
If set, used as the current time instead of the clock: a full stamp such as
.B 09761.4564
or
.BI @ SECONDS
since the Unix epoch.  It is meant for tests.

[FILES]
.TP
.I ~/.config/daykeep/config
Settings, one
.I "key = value"
per line;
.B #
starts a comment.  The only key is
.BR epoch ,
day 0, as for
.BR \-\-epoch .
The file is
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/daykeep/config
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set.

[SEE ALSO]
.BR date (1)
